add(2008,3,4,'The largest prime divisor rules out every prime above 3, after which divisibility of the square-free-divisor sum leaves only three integers.','When a square-free divisor sum is a multiple',r'Let $F(n)$ be the sum of the square-free positive divisors of $n$. Determine all positive integers $n$ for which $F(n)/n$ is an integer.',r'If the distinct prime factors of $n$ are $p_1,\ldots,p_k$, then $F(n)=\prod(p_i+1)$. Suppose the largest factor is $p>3$. Since $p\mid n\mid F(n)$, it divides $q+1$ for some prime factor $q\le p$. The only possibility is $q+1=p$, but then $q=p-1$ is even and larger than 2, a contradiction. Thus $n=2^a3^b$. If exactly one exponent is positive, $F(n)$ is respectively 3 or 4 and is not divisible by $n$. If both are positive, $F(n)=12$, so $n$ is 6 or 12. Also $n=1$ works.','Number Theory',['Divisibility','Primes','Arithmetic functions'],source=s)
add(2008,4,6,'A complete irreducibility analysis needs both possible factor degrees; coefficient elimination in the quadratic–cubic case interacts with the prime-power constant term.','Reducibility of a quintic with prime-power constant',r'Find all primes $p$ and positive integers $n$ for which $x^5+x+p^n$ factors into two integer polynomials of positive degree.',r'The sole pair is $(p,n)=(2,1)$. For a linear factor with root $t\in\mathbb Z$, $t(t^4+1)=-p^n$ and the two factors are coprime, forcing $t=-1$ and $p^n=2$. Otherwise monicity permits a quadratic factor $x^2+ax+b$. Comparing coefficients with its monic cubic quotient gives $3a^2b-a^4-b^2=1$ and $ab(2b-a^2)=p^n$. Thus $a,b$ are signed powers of $p$, but cannot both be divisible by $p$; one is $\pm1$. If $a=\pm1$, the first identity gives $b=1$ or 2, and the constant term is $\pm1$ or $\pm6$, impossible. If $b=\pm1$, the same identity forces $a=\pm1$, already excluded. Finally $x^5+x+2=(x+1)(x^4-x^3+x^2-x+2)$.','Algebra',['Polynomials','Irreducibility','Coefficient comparison','Prime powers'],source=s)
add(2008,5,2.5,'The number of trailing zeros is v5(m!); its jump at a sufficiently large power of 5 skips 2007 consecutive values.',source=s)
add(2008,6,3.5,'The two 45-degree angles put P on the circle with diameter CD; its other intersection with AB lies on the perpendicular bisector.',source=s)
add(2008,7,1.5,'Apply the triangle inequality to each within-triangle side through all three vertices of the other triangle and add.',source=s)
add(2008,8,4,'Double-counting agreements gives the lower bound; a balanced collection of the ten two-red column patterns attains the rounded bound.','Two rows with many matching colours',r'Every cell of a $5\times2008$ board is coloured red or blue. What is the largest integer $N$ such that two rows always agree in at least $N$ columns?',r'The answer is 804. A column with $r$ red cells contributes $\binom r2+\binom{5-r}2\ge4$ agreements among the ten row pairs. Thus some pair has at least $\lceil4\cdot2008/10\rceil=804$ agreements. For sharpness, use 200 copies of each of the ten column patterns with exactly two red cells. Every pair agrees in exactly four of the ten patterns, so initially has 800 agreements. Append any eight different such patterns. A pair can gain at most four agreements, hence no pair exceeds 804.','Combinatorics',['Double counting','Pigeonhole principle','Colouring','Constructions'],source=s)
