add(2010,3,4.5,'Resolve lengths along the base and express the two perpendicular cevians by the tangent and cotangent of one angle; a sine difference cancels the apparent complexity.','A median, bisector and two perpendiculars',r'In triangle $ABC$ with $AB\ne AC$, points $P,Q,R,S$ lie on line $BC$: $P$ is its midpoint, $AQ$ bisects $\angle BAC$, $AR\perp BC$, and $AS\perp AQ$. Prove $PR\cdot QS=AB\cdot AC$.',r'Take $R=(0,0),A=(0,h),B=(b,0),C=(c,0)$ with $h>0$ and $b<c$. Put $u=AB,v=AC$. If $\theta$ is the directed angle from $AR$ to $AQ$, the angle-bisector property gives $2\theta=\arctan(b/h)+\arctan(c/h)$. Thus $|\sin2\theta|=h|b+c|/(uv)$. Also $PR=|b+c|/2$. The intersections of the two perpendicular lines $AQ,AS$ with the base have coordinates $h\tan\theta$ and $-h\cot\theta$, so $QS=2h/|\sin2\theta|$. Multiplication yields $uv$. The assumption $AB\ne AC$ ensures $b+c\ne0$.','Geometry',['Trigonometry','Angle bisectors','Medians','Coordinates'],source=s)
add(2010,4,None,'Prime classification with square-free remainders read; a complete bound on candidate primes remains pending.',source=s)
add(2010,5,3,'Resolve the point vector along the two perpendicular chords; the two quadratic-root sums give the constant 2(r²+s²).',source=s)
add(2010,6,3.5,'Each nested cube root is a sum of two simple cube roots: the four unordered numerator pairs are {13,5},{11,7},{7,5},{13,11}, all over 3.',source=s)
add(2010,7,4.5,'Four failed maximal-divisor tests label four distinct edges of K4; a cycle gives a contradiction after reduction modulo the prime corresponding to one edge.','A divisor that separates four residues',r'Let $0<a<b<c<d<2010$ be integers. Prove that some positive divisor $e<2010$ of 2010 makes their four remainders pairwise distinct.',r'Use the four divisors $2010/p$ for $p\in\{2,3,5,67\}$. If each fails, choose a pair of the four numbers whose difference is divisible by that divisor, and label its edge by $p$. No edge can receive two labels: the least common multiple of the corresponding divisors is 2010, larger than every nonzero difference. Four distinct edges on four vertices contain a cycle. Sum the signed differences around that cycle; the sum is zero. Choose an edge labelled $p$. Every other edge difference on the cycle is divisible by $p$, whereas this edge difference is $(2010/p)t$ with $0<|t|<p$ and so is not divisible by $p$. This contradicts the zero sum.','Number Theory',['Divisibility','Congruences','Graph theory','Primes'],source=s)
