add(2011,3,4,'Translate the strict distance inequalities into a common open half-plane through the circumcentre; the semicircle criterion is then equivalent to obtuseness.','Three vertices closer than the circumradius',r'Three distinct points $A,B,C$ lie on a circle of radius $r$. Prove that their triangle is obtuse exactly when there is a point $X$ with $XA,XB,XC<r$.',r'Place the centre at the origin. If such $X$ exists, then for each vertex vector $v$, $|v-X|^2<r^2=|v|^2$, so $v\cdot X>|X|^2/2>0$. All vertices therefore lie in an open semicircle. Conversely, if all lie in an open semicircle, choose a unit vector $u$ pointing into its half-plane. The three numbers $v\cdot u$ have a positive minimum; for sufficiently small $t>0$, $X=tu$ satisfies $|v-tu|^2=r^2-2t(v\cdot u)+t^2<r^2$ for all vertices. Finally, three points lie in an open semicircle exactly when the intervening opposite arc has length greater than a semicircle; the inscribed angle subtending it is then greater than $90^\circ$.','Geometry',['Circles','Vectors','Geometric inequalities','Angles'],source=s)
add(2011,4,5.5,'Initial sum-of-two-squares identities establish a substantial base range; two further identities then support a strong induction for all inputs.','A function preserving sums of two squares',r'Determine all $f:\mathbb Z_{\ge0}\to\mathbb Z_{\ge0}$ with $f(1)>0$ and $f(x)^2+f(y)^2=f(x^2+y^2)$ for nonnegative integers $x,y$.',r'The unique answer is $f(n)=n$. Substituting $(0,0),(1,0),(1,1)$ gives $f(0)=0,f(1)=1,f(2)=2$, then $f(4)=4,f(5)=5,f(8)=8$. Whenever $t^2+u^2=v^2+w^2$ and $f$ fixes $u,v,w$, it fixes $t$, since its values are nonnegative. Use $3^2+4^2=0^2+5^2$ to get $f(3)=3$, then $f(9)=9,f(10)=10$. Next $6^2+8^2=0^2+10^2$ and $7^2+1^2=5^2+5^2$ give 6 and 7. For strong induction beyond 10, use $(2m+1)^2+(m-2)^2=(2m-1)^2+(m+2)^2$ for odd inputs, and $(2m+2)^2+(m-4)^2=(2m-2)^2+(m+4)^2$ for even inputs. All three other arguments are smaller and nonnegative. The identity function directly works.','Algebra',['Functional equations','Induction','Sum of squares'],source=s)
add(2011,5,None,'Inscribed rhombus and midpoint line read; perpendicularity proof remains pending.',source=s)
add(2011,6,3,'Vieta gives perimeter and product; the right-angle identity reduces the hypotenuse to a quadratic, selecting the 5–12–13 triangle.',source=s)
add(2011,7,4,'An odd prime factor creates a two-to-one triangular-residue obstruction, whereas shifting an index by a power of two lifts either residue class.','Triangular numbers covering every residue',r'Find all positive integers $n$ such that for every integer $k$ there is an integer $i$ with $i(i-1)/2\equiv k\pmod n$.',r'The answers are the powers of 2, including 1. If an odd prime $p$ divides $n$, then modulo $p$ the indices $i$ and $1-i$ give the same triangular number, so there are at most $(p+1)/2<p$ values. Thus no such prime may divide $n$. Conversely, induct on $a$ to obtain all residues modulo $2^a$. Given an index with the desired residue modulo $2^{a-1}$, compare it with $i+2^a$: their triangular numbers differ by $2^{a-1}(2i+2^a-1)$, which is $2^{a-1}$ modulo $2^a$. These two choices give both possible lifts.','Number Theory',['Modular arithmetic','Induction','Triangular numbers','Primes'],source=s)
add(2011,8,4,'The smallest selected number bounds a translate tightly enough that two sets of size 2011 must overlap; the overlap yields ten repeated summands.','One element as a sum of eleven selected integers',r'Let $T\subseteq\{1,2,\ldots,2211\}$ have 2011 elements. Prove that some element of $T$ is a sum of 11 elements of $T$, with repetition allowed.',r'Let $m=\min T$. Since only 200 integers are omitted, $m\le201$. Both $T$ and its translate $T+10m$ lie in the integer interval $[m,2211+10m]$, containing $2212+9m\le4021$ integers. Their sizes add to 4022, so they intersect. Therefore $x+10m\in T$ for some $x\in T$. This is the sum of $x$ and ten copies of $m$, all selected elements.','Combinatorics',['Pigeonhole principle','Sets','Translations','Extremal arguments'],source=s)
