add(2012,2,4.5,'Discard at most two diagonal points, then classify pairwise-intersecting two-element coordinate sets as a star or a triangle; a generic construction attains eight.','Points whose coordinate pairs overlap',r'A set $S$ of points in the plane has no three collinear. For every pair $(a,b),(c,d)$ in $S$, at least two of $a,b,c,d$ are equal. What is the largest possible size of $S$?',r'The maximum is 8. At most two points lie on $x=y$. Each remaining point gives a two-element coordinate set, and these sets intersect pairwise. Such a family either has one element common to all sets, or consists of subsets of a fixed three-element set: take two sets $\{u,v\},\{u,w\}$; a set missing $u$ must be $\{v,w\}$, which forces the claim. In the common-element case, the points lie on one horizontal and one vertical line, so there are at most four. Otherwise there are at most six ordered pairs. Add the two diagonal points to get the bound 8. For attainment, take all six ordered pairs of distinct elements of $\{0,1,3\}$; direct checking shows no three collinear. Add two points on $x=y$ avoiding the finitely many intersections with lines through existing pairs.','Combinatorics',['Sets','Combinatorial geometry','Extremal arguments','Constructions'],source=s)
add(2012,3,None,'Tangent-circle quadrilateral with prescribed side ratios read; complete angle proof is pending.',source=s)
add(2012,4,5,'Divisibility by two root differences severely restricts any non-root preimage; the remaining many integer factors then contradict its small value.','Integer zeros after composing a split polynomial',r'A monic polynomial $p$ of degree 2012 has zero constant term and 2012 distinct integer roots. Find all integers $n$ for which $p(p(n))=0$.',r'Every root $n$ works since $p(0)=0$. Conversely suppose $n$ is not a root and put $r=p(n)$, which must be a nonzero root. As 0 and $r$ are roots, $n\mid r$ and $n-r\mid r$. Write $r=kn$. The second divisibility gives $1-k\mid k$, hence $k=0$ or 2; only $k=2$ is possible. The two factors $n$ and $n-r=-n$ in $p(n)$ show $n^2\mid2n$, so $|n|$ is 1 or 2. But the remaining 2010 factors are nonzero distinct integers; their absolute product exceeds 2. This contradicts $|p(n)|=2|n|$. Therefore precisely the original 2012 roots work.','Algebra',['Integer polynomials','Factor theorem','Divisibility','Polynomial composition'],source=s)
add(2012,5,3,'Equal products force x=5z,y=3z; the positive right-hand side gives z>4, where 15(z+4)²(z−4) increases strictly.',source=s)
add(2012,6,4.5,'Separate equal exponents, then apply valuations to a difference of cubes; the gcd of its factors leaves only the exceptional prime 7.','Two powers of one prime adding to a cube',r'Find all triples $(p,a,b)$ with $p$ prime and positive integers $a\le b$ such that $p^a+p^b$ is a perfect cube.',r'If $a=b$, the sum is $2p^a$, so $p=2$ and $a=b=3k-1$ for $k\ge1$. If $a<b$, the factors $p^a$ and $1+p^{b-a}$ are coprime. Thus $a=3k$ and $1+p^{b-a}=t^3$ for an integer $t\ge2$. Factor $p^{b-a}=(t-1)(t^2+t+1)$; their gcd divides 3. For $p\ne3$, they are coprime powers of the same prime, forcing $t-1=1$. Hence $t=2,p=7,b-a=1$. For $p=3$, we must have $t\equiv1\pmod3$; writing $t=1+3u$ shows $v_3(t^2+t+1)=1$. That factor, itself a power of 3, would then equal 3, impossible for $t\ge2$. The remaining family is $(p,a,b)=(7,3k,3k+1)$.','Number Theory',['Valuations','Diophantine equations','Difference of cubes','GCD'],source=s)
add(2012,7,4.5,'A parity count of red-blue edges in a triangulated dual grid forces a tricoloured triangle, which lies in four original tiles meeting at a point.','Three colours meeting inside a rectangle',r'A rectangular array has at least three rows and columns. Its bottom row is red and top row green. Its left edge is blue except for the top and bottom tiles; its right edge is green except for the bottom tile. All other tiles are red, blue or green. Prove some four tiles meeting at one point contain all three colours.',r'Form the grid whose vertices are tile centres and triangulate every elementary square with one diagonal. Along the boundary there is exactly one red-blue edge, on the left near the bottom. Count incidences between triangles and red-blue edges modulo 2. Internal edges occur twice, so the total is odd. A triangle with only red and blue vertices has either zero or two such edges; a triangle containing all three colours has exactly one. Therefore some triangle is tricoloured. Its three vertices are centres of tiles among one block of four tiles, which meet at the required point.','Combinatorics',['Colouring','Parity','Double counting','Sperner lemma','Combinatorial geometry'],source=s)
